{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>Because you are my brother, shall you serve me for nothing?</b> Scripture did not relate that Jacob had been serving him. It is possible that from the time it says, \"and he watered the flock of Laban his mother's brother\" (Genesis 29:10), the flock did not leave his hand, for when he saw Rachel, that she was a shepherdess, he took pity on her so that she would not return to herd sheep again, and he would tend them out of his love for her. And it is further possible to say that Laban spoke with cunning: at first he told him that he is his own flesh and blood, and he would have compassion on him just as a person has compassion on himself and on his own flesh. And when he saw that Jacob was lingering there, being sustained from what belonged to Laban, he said to him, \"Because you are my brother, shall you serve me for nothing?\" — for I know that from now on you will serve me, for you are a disciplined man and will not be sustained from others' property, and I too do not wish the labor you do for me to be for nothing without full wages, so tell me what you seek as your wages and I will give it. Then Jacob recognized his intention, and told him that he would serve him seven years for Rachel. And the labor, by implication, is tending the flock, for that is what they needed and that is what they spoke of."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 29, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:36:45.445835264Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}